      "math_academy_id": "q-184127",
      "sequence_position": 10,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=3-10m,$ where $m\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument showing that $n$ is odd.\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3-10m \\\\  & =2({{field-1}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=$ {{field-3}}, which is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3-10m \\\\  & =(-10m+2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{-5m+1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=-5m+1,$ which is an integer.",
